Agreed, and that model is better than mine. Rebasing onto my outbox
makes your list depend on my line, which is why the race needed a
merge at all. A fix that follows your model is posted as "[PATCH]
mail: Copy Claude's outbox onto @ in just fetch" [1].

> Maybe another way of thinking of this is, instead of rebasing or merging
> commits from claude@submissions, I just duplicate all of the commits
> from claude@submissions that aren't in ::@ ?

Yes, that works. The recipe is now:

    jj --quiet git fetch --remote submissions
    jj --quiet duplicate -B @ '::claude@submissions ~ ::@'
    jj --quiet abandon 'empty() & mutable() & ::@ ~ mine()'

-B @ puts the copies between @- and @, so the work in @ stays on top
and `just advance` picks the copies up as before.

> What I don't know is if there's a way to --skip-emptied for a jj
> duplicate,

There isn't one in jj 0.45.1. Only rebase has --skip-emptied. But a
copy of a commit you already applied comes out empty, since it adds
files @ already has. So the second line drops empty mutable commits
under @ that someone else wrote. Your own empty commits, @ included,
are left alone.

> or identify the changes / commits that are in
> claude@submissions and need to be cherry-picked into working copy.

`::claude@submissions ~ ::@` is close enough. It also matches
commits you already cherry-picked, because a copy has a new commit
id. Those are the ones that come out empty. Each of my passes rebases
my outbox onto mail@upstream, which drops what you applied, so the
set stays small.

One gap: if you edit a message while applying it, the next fetch
copies my original again, and that conflicts with your edit. It lasts
until my next pass drops the applied commit. The patch notes have
this and the scratch-repo tests: the race, a rerun, the usual case,
and an outbox that is already fully under @.

